 ## Resolved Technical Debt
 
+### 2026-09-14 — A failed `make docs-widoco` could re-baseline WebVOWL output to raw generator bytes
+
+**Resolved Date**: 2026-09-14
+
+**What it was**: `docs-widoco` snapshots the working-tree `docs/webvowl/data/ontology.json` as the
+normalizer baseline, then `rsync` overwrites that same path with fresh WIDOCO output before the
+normalizer runs. A failure after the rsync — which since 2026-08-16 fails loudly rather than
+silently — left the working tree holding raw generator bytes. The next `make docs-widoco` then
+adopted those raw bytes as its baseline, found the fresh raw output semantically equal to them,
+restored them, and went green over un-normalized output. Local only: CI checks out clean, so its
+baseline is always the committed file.
+
+**Resolution**: the recipe keeps the working-tree baseline and arms a shell `trap … EXIT` that puts
+the pre-run bytes of `docs/webvowl/data/ontology.json` back on any non-zero exit. It can restore them
+exactly, because the baseline copy the recipe already takes *is* those bytes — the property that made
+the defect possible is the one that makes the repair free. The trap is disarmed as soon as the
+normalizer succeeds, since from that point the file holds normalized output and every remaining
+command is cleanup. It does not cover a shell killed by a signal, where no `EXIT` trap runs at all and
+`git checkout -- docs/webvowl/data/ontology.json` is still the repair. Decision, alternative and
+verification: [ADR-010](adr/010-docs-widoco-restores-prerun-webvowl-baseline.md).
+
+**The `Intended fix` this entry used to carry was rejected, and is recorded here rather than
+deleted** so that the repository is not left teaching a discarded option. That intended fix was to
+take the baseline from `git show HEAD:docs/webvowl/data/ontology.json` — the fallback
+`scripts/normalize_webvowl_json.py` already implements in `load_git_head_text` — instead of from the
+working-tree copy. Brett ruled against it on 2026-09-14. The working-tree baseline is deliberate: it
+is what makes repeated local refreshes compare against the previous local run rather than against the
+last commit, and closing a failure window by discarding a wanted property is the wrong trade when a
+trap closes the same window and keeps it. ADR-010 has the full comparison.
+
+**Lessons Learned**: when one file is both the baseline a later step compares against and the target
+an earlier step overwrites, a failure between the two does not merely fail — it silently changes what
+"unchanged" means for the next run, so the second run is the one that reports success. The repair
+belongs in the failure path rather than in the comparison. Note also which half of this the
+2026-08-16 `set -e` fix bought: it made the failure visible, and a visible failure that still leaves
+its output in the tree is exactly what this entry then described.
+
